QB.add(59,[{"n":1,"q":"The ophthalmic examination of a 32-year-old woman reveals pale optic discs and a relative afferent pupillary defect. She is diagnosed with bilateral optic neuritis secondary to neuromyelitis optica (NMO). Which of the following is not a typical feature of bilateral optic neuritis in NMO?","o":[{"t":"Eye pain, especially with eye movement"},{"t":"Central or paracentral scotomas"},{"t":"Unilateral involvement of the optic nerve"},{"t":"Decreased visual acuity"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Unilateral involvement of the optic nerve"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NMO characteristically presents with bilateral optic neuritis , meaning both optic nerves are affected. Unilateral optic neuritis is more common in multiple sclerosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMO)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NMO, formerly Devic’s disease , involves: Transverse myelitis : Inflammatory spinal cord lesion. Bilateral optic neuritis : Inflammation in both optic nerves."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transverse myelitis : Inflammatory spinal cord lesion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral optic neuritis : Inflammation in both optic nerves."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aquaporin-4 antibodies are often detected in most cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain MRI may show lesions near the periventricular region."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal MRI reveals lesions extending across ‡3 spinal segments (longer than MS lesions)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical recovery in NMO tends to be worse compared to MS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NMO shows a more aggressive course with frequent relapses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment includes glucocorticoids, azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, or plasmapheresis for better outcomes than MS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transverse myelitis : Inflammatory spinal cord lesion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral optic neuritis : Inflammation in both optic nerves."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Transverse Myelitis in NMO"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Domain Symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sensory Numbness, tingling, or cold sensations in limbs"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Often progresses proximally (up to the trunk)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Motor Weakness, stiffness, or heaviness in limbs (mostly legs)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sphincters Urgency or difficulty in urination"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"This can lead to urinary retention"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Bilateral Optic Neuritis in NMO"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptom Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vision Loss Partial or complete loss in one or both eyes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Visual Acuity Decrease Blurry or reduced sharpness of vision (Option D ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Reduced Color Perception Diminished ability to see colours accurately"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Optic Atrophy Pale optic disc due to nerve fibre loss"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Afferent Pupillary Defect Delayed pupil response in the affected eye"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pain with Eye Movement Eye pain, especially with central vision loss (Option A"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Scotoma Central or paracentral blind spots in vision (Option B"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out)"}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with asymmetric muscle weakness in the arms and legs. Nerve conduction studies reveal focal demyelination with conduction block. Which of the following conditions is most likely indicated by these findings?","o":[{"t":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)"},{"t":"Multifocal Motor Neuropathy with Conduction Block (MMN)"},{"t":"Diabetic Polyneuropathy"},{"t":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth Disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Multifocal Motor Neuropathy with Conduction Block (MMN)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multifocal Motor Neuropathy with Conduction Block (MMN) is confirmed through nerve conduction studies that reveal focal demyelination and conduction blocks, differentiating it from conditions like Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS), which typically show normal conduction studies. MMN commonly presents with asymmetric muscle weakness, particularly affecting the arms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multifocal Neuropathy with Conduction Block:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition: Multifocal neuropathy, also known as mononeuritis multiplex, involves damage to multiple nerve roots, peripheral nerves, or cranial nerves."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characteristics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pattern of Involvement: Affects multiple individual nerves rather than a symmetrical distribution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects multiple individual nerves rather than a symmetrical distribution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multifocal Motor Neuropathy with Conduction Block (MMN): Specific subtypes affecting motor nerves are identifiable via nerve conduction studies. Typically, it affects arms more than legs. About 50% of cases are associated with antibodies against ganglioside GM1. Usually responds to intravenous immunoglobulin treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Specific subtypes affecting motor nerves are identifiable via nerve conduction studies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically, it affects arms more than legs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"About 50% of cases are associated with antibodies against ganglioside GM1."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Usually responds to intravenous immunoglobulin treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects multiple individual nerves rather than a symmetrical distribution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Specific subtypes affecting motor nerves are identifiable via nerve conduction studies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically, it affects arms more than legs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"About 50% of cases are associated with antibodies against ganglioside GM1."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Usually responds to intravenous immunoglobulin treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Distinguishing MMN from ALS:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria MMN ALS"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nerve Conduct Shows focal demyelination Normal sensory and motor ion Studies with/without conduction block conduction studies; may show"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"reduced motor action potential amplitude"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms Weakness in specific muscles; Progressive muscle weakness; can"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"asymmetrical be symmetrical or asymmetrical"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Multifocal Neuropathy:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Potential Causes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Axonal Multifocal Neuropathy Vasculitis (systemic or non-systemic)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diabetes mellitus Sarcoidosis Infections (HIV, hepatitis C, Lyme disease, leprosy, diphtheria)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Demyelinating Multifocal Neuropathy Multifocal motor neuropathy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Multiple compression neuropathies Multifocal acquired demyelinating sensory and motor neuropathy (MADSAM) Hereditary neuropathy with a predisposition to pressure palsy Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Points:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MMN can mimic ALS but is distinguishable through nerve conduction studies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment options are available, notably intravenous immunoglobulin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Various underlying conditions can lead to multifocal neuropathy, either axonal or demyelinating in nature."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) (Option A): Typically shows normal sensory and motor nerve conduction studies, so this option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diabetic Polyneuropathy (Option C): Generally presents with a symmetrical pattern of involvement and does not typically show conduction blocks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth Disease (Option D): A hereditary neuropathy that primarily presents with a different pattern of symptoms and nerve conduction abnormalities, usually not associated with acute conduction blocks."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 3-year-old child diagnosed with Spinal Muscular Atrophy (SMA), which of the following symptoms is not typically associated with SMA?","o":[{"t":"Muscle weakness in distal limbs"},{"t":"Scoliosis"},{"t":"Joint contractures"},{"t":"Respiratory infections"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Muscle weakness in distal limbs"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SMA primarily affects proximal muscles more than distal ones."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spinal Muscular Atrophy (SMA):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Genetic Basis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common Cause: Caused by mutations in the SMN1 gene , leading to insufficient survival motor neuron (SMN) protein , essential for motor neuron health."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Forms: Different genes may be involved in other SMA forms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle Weakness: Primarily affects trunk and proximal limbs more than distal muscles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additional Issues: Respiratory infections (Option D ruled out) Scoliosis (Option B ruled out) Joint contractures (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory infections (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scoliosis (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Joint contractures (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory infections (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scoliosis (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Joint contractures (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Method Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Evaluation Thorough medical history, physical examination, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neurological assessment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic Testing Blood tests identify mutations in the SMN1 gene,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"detecting ~95% of SMA cases."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Electromyography (EMG) Assesses muscle and nerve function."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nerve Conduction Studies Evaluates the electrical activity of muscles and nerves."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment Description"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Supportive Care Aims to optimise quality of life; includes:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Physical therapy Nutritional support Respiratory care"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FDA-Approved Treatments Nusinersen (Spinraza):"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Increases SMN protein production by modifying splicing of the SMN2 gene. Significant improvements in motor function for SMA types I, II, and III. Other emerging therapies are under investigation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Onasemnogene Abeparvovec ( Zolgensma): It is a gene therapy that delivers a functional copy of the SMN1 gene to motor neurons, enabling the production of the SMN protein. It is approved for children under 2 years with SMA. This treatment has been shown to enhance muscle movement, function, and overall survival."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risdiplam (Evrysdi) It is an oral medication that increases the production of SMN protein in the body. It is approved for the treatment of SMA in adults and children aged 2 months and older ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Research Initiatives:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The NIH focuses on: Identifying genetic mutations contributing to SMA. Exploring cellular processes for potential therapeutic targets. Developing biomarkers for better disease monitoring and treatment efficacy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Identifying genetic mutations contributing to SMA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exploring cellular processes for potential therapeutic targets."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Developing biomarkers for better disease monitoring and treatment efficacy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Identifying genetic mutations contributing to SMA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exploring cellular processes for potential therapeutic targets."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Developing biomarkers for better disease monitoring and treatment efficacy."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 65-year-old woman with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) is experiencing significant difficulty swallowing and has been advised to consider a feeding intervention. Which treatment option is most appropriate for enhancing her quality of life and potentially extending survival?","o":[{"t":"Riluzole"},{"t":"Highly selective vagotomy"},{"t":"Percutaneous Gastrostomy (PEG)"},{"t":"Palliative Care"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Percutaneous Gastrostomy (PEG)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Percutaneous Gastrostomy (PEG) enhances the quality of life related to feeding and can potentially extend survival, especially in advanced stages."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"No cure for ALS: Management focuses on symptom relief and supportive care."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Riluzole (Option A ruled Glutamate release antagonist. out) Prolongs median survival by 2–3 months."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Non-Invasive Ventilation Significantly prolongs survival and improves quality of (NIV) life."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Best for those with better baseline bulbar function."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Percutaneous Enhances quality of life and may extend survival. Gastrostomy (PEG) Effective even in later disease stages."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multidisciplinary Care Involves a team of specialists:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Physiotherapists, speech and occupational therapists, dietitians, neurologists, respiratory specialists, and palliative care teams."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Importance of Early Access to Palliative Care:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapid access to palliative care is crucial as patients transition into terminal phases of MND. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Additional Supportive Measures:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Psychological Support: Emotional support through counselling and support groups for patients and families."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Practical Assistance: Help with daily activities and mobility through occupational therapists and social workers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Financial and Legal Planning: Guidance on estate planning and disability benefits to manage financial strain due to medical expenses."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Highly selective vagotomy (Option B): This surgical procedure is not typically used in ALS management and is primarily associated with peptic ulcer disease, not swallowing difficulties in ALS."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with muscle weakness, fasciculations, and difficulty swallowing. Electromyography (EMG) shows evidence of widespread denervation and reinnervation. Which of the following tests is least useful in diagnosing Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)?","o":[{"t":"Electromyography (EMG)"},{"t":"Genetic testing"},{"t":"Spinal fluid analysis"},{"t":"Nerve conduction studies"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Spinal fluid analysis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spinal fluid analysis is not commonly required for diagnosing ALS, as it does not provide specific diagnostic information and is mainly used to rule out other conditions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electromyography (EMG): Confirms denervation and reinnervation. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nerve Conduction Studies: Normal, though motor action potential amplitude may be reduced. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creatine Kinase: Mildly elevated but not specific."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal Fluid Analysis: Typically not required."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic Testing: Identifies mutations (SOD1, FUS, TARDBP, C9orf72) in familial cases. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging (MRI): Used to exclude structural lesions or other conditions mimicking ALS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Differential Diagnoses:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multifocal Motor Neuropathy with Conduction Block: Nerve conduction studies show conduction blocks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kennedy's Disease: Involves sensory disturbances and endocrine abnormalities, unlike ALS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cervical Spondylotic Myelopathy: Spinal cord compression leads to weakness and sensory changes; imaging helps differentiate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syringomyelia: Spinal cord cyst causing weakness and sensory loss; MRI distinguishes from ALS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conclusion: Diagnosis of ALS is based on clinical evaluation, EMG findings, and the exclusion of other conditions. There's no single definitive test for ALS."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 58-year-old woman presents with muscle weakness in her hands and frequent cramping in her legs. She also reports difficulty swallowing and slurred speech. O/E, she has muscle wasting, fasciculations in her arms, and increased muscle tone. Which of the following signs or symptoms is not typically associated with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)?","o":[{"t":"Fasciculations"},{"t":"Dysphagia"},{"t":"Sensory loss"},{"t":"Muscle wasting"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Sensory loss"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ALS does not typically involve sensory deficits; sensory function remains intact."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Onset and Epidemiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Onset usually after age 50, with an average age of 65"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rare before age 30"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More common in males"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle wasting, often with cramps and fasciculations (Options A&D; ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Limb weakness, manifesting as foot drop or difficulty with manual tasks"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar symptoms: dysarthria (difficulty speaking) and dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory failure in terminal stages (respiratory onset is rare)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive and behavioural changes resembling frontotemporal dementia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No sensory, autonomic, or visual symptoms (cramps are frequent) The presence of significant sensory loss is a distinguishing feature of Subacute Combined Degeneration (SACD) compared to ALS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of significant sensory loss is a distinguishing feature of Subacute Combined Degeneration (SACD) compared to ALS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of significant sensory loss is a distinguishing feature of Subacute Combined Degeneration (SACD) compared to ALS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Signs:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lower Motor Neuron Signs: Muscle wasting and fasciculations Weakness in limbs, tongue, face, and palate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle wasting and fasciculations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness in limbs, tongue, face, and palate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Upper Motor Neuron Signs: Spasticity, exaggerated reflexes, extensor plantar response"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spasticity, exaggerated reflexes, extensor plantar response"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No involvement of external ocular muscles or sphincters"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive impairment in 50% (10% develop frontotemporal dementia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle wasting and fasciculations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness in limbs, tongue, face, and palate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spasticity, exaggerated reflexes, extensor plantar response"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Disease Course:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms start in one area and spread"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relentless progression despite treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Specialist care improves median survival (non-invasive ventilation, feeding, pharmacological interventions)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patterns of Motor Neuron Involvement:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Most common; combined upper and lower motor neuron (ALS) signs"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Progressive Muscular Atrophy Predominantly lower motor neuron involvement"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Progressive Lateral Sclerosis Primarily affects upper motor neurons"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Progressive Bulbar Palsy Early involvement of tongue, palate, and pharyngeal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"muscles (dysarthria, dysphagia)"}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with progressive weakness in his limbs, accompanied by muscle wasting and dysarthria. Neurological examination reveals both upper and lower motor neuron signs. Based on the clinical features, which of the following conditions is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Progressive Muscular Atrophy"},{"t":"Progressive Lateral Sclerosis"},{"t":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis"},{"t":"Progressive Bulbar Palsy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis is the most common type of motor neuron disease, featuring both upper and lower motor neuron signs, consistent with the patient's symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of Motor Neuron Disease (MND)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type of MND Motor Neuron Involvement Key Symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Both upper and lower motor neurons Most common form of MND."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Weakness and muscle wasting"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Progressive Muscular Atrophy Predominantly lower motor neurons Weakness and wasting of distal (Option A ruled out) Described as a \" pure lower motor limb muscles"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neuron \" variant."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Progressive Lateral Sclerosis Primarily upper motor neurons Spasticity, few lower motor (Option B ruled out) Characterized as a \" pure upper neuron signs"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"motor neuron \" variant."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Progressive Bulbar Palsy (Option D Early involvement of cranial nerves Dysarthria, dysphagia ruled out)"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A genetic study of a family with a history of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) reveals a mutation in the SOD1 gene. Which of the following statements about this mutation is correct?","o":[{"t":"SOD1 mutations account for approximately 50% of all familial ALS cases"},{"t":"The SOD1 gene is responsible for the majority of sporadic ALS cases"},{"t":"Mutations in the SOD1 gene are linked to oxidative stress in motor neurons"},{"t":"SOD1 is linked to MND and frontotemporal dementia."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Mutations in the SOD1 gene are linked to oxidative stress in motor neurons."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SOD1 mutations lead to oxidative stress and neurotoxicity, contributing to motor neuron degeneration in ALS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) Pathophysiology"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition and Incidence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) , also known as Motor Neuron Disease (MND) , is a neurodegenerative disorder characterised by the degeneration of motor neurons in: Spinal cord Cranial nerve nuclei Motor cortex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal cord"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial nerve nuclei"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor cortex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incidence : Approximately 2 per 100,000 annually"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prevalence : Approximately 7 per 100,000"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types : Majority are sporadic About 10% are familial , indicating a genetic link"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Majority are sporadic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"About 10% are familial , indicating a genetic link"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal cord"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial nerve nuclei"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor cortex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Majority are sporadic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"About 10% are familial , indicating a genetic link"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Potential Causes and Contributing Factors"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Factor Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic Mutation Abnormalities in the SOD1 gene account for ~20% of s familial ALS cases. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"C9orf72 gene is linked to MND and frontotemporal dementia. (Option D ruled out) Importance of Testing : Genetic tests for SOD1, FUS, TARDBP, and C9orf72 help assess disease risk in families."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Environmental Potential role of toxins in neurodegenerative diseases Toxins like ALS, though no definitive environmental toxin has"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"been identified."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other Factors The precise cause of sporadic ALS is still unknown."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathological Features"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neuron Loss Progressive degeneration of upper and lower motor"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neurons in the brain and spinal cord."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Protein Inclusions The presence of protein aggregates may contribute to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ALS pathogenesis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impact of Neuron Loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle Weakness and Wasting : Disruption of signals to muscles causes weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spasticity : Loss of upper motor neurons results in increased muscle tone and exaggerated reflexes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar Symptoms : Affects cranial nerve nuclei, leading to dysarthria (speech difficulties) and dysphagia (swallowing difficulties)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory Failure : Progressive weakness of respiratory muscles can lead to respiratory failure, a common cause of death in ALS."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with acute vision loss and limb weakness. She is diagnosed with Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD). Which of the following treatments is most commonly used to manage acute attacks of NMOSD?","o":[{"t":"Azathioprine"},{"t":"Plasmapheresis"},{"t":"Cyclophosphamide"},{"t":"Monoclonal antibodies"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Plasmapheresis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plasmapheresis is specifically utilised to treat severe acute attacks of Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD) by removing pathogenic antibodies from the blood, thereby reducing inflammation and preventing further damage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment Options for NMOSD"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Treatments:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucocorticoids Used to reduce inflammation during acute attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used to reduce inflammation during acute attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine Immunosuppressant that helps prevent relapses. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunosuppressant that helps prevent relapses. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide Immunosuppressant for severe cases or when other treatments fail. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunosuppressant for severe cases or when other treatments fail. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasmapheresis Procedure to remove antibodies from the blood, used for severe attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Procedure to remove antibodies from the blood, used for severe attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used to reduce inflammation during acute attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunosuppressant that helps prevent relapses. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunosuppressant for severe cases or when other treatments fail. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Procedure to remove antibodies from the blood, used for severe attacks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Emerging Treatments"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Type Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monoclonal Antibodie Targeted therapies block specific immune components s involved in NMOSD. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complement Inhibitor Medications that target the complement system to s reduce inflammation and damage."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 30-year-old female experiences bilateral vision loss and lower limb weakness. MRI of the spinal cord shows lesions spanning more than three segments. What is the most specific test for diagnosing Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD)?","o":[{"t":"Presence of oligoclonal bands in CSF"},{"t":"AQP4-IgG antibody testing"},{"t":"Evoked potential studies"},{"t":"Brain MRI findings"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) AQP4-IgG antibody testing"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of AQP4-IgG (aquaporin 4) antibodies is a highly specific biomarker for Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD), distinguishing it from other demyelinating diseases like multiple sclerosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This test effectively confirms the diagnosis in patients exhibiting characteristic clinical features, such as bilateral optic neuritis and transverse myelitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis and Investigations of Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features: Characterized by transverse myelitis and bilateral optic neuritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characterized by transverse myelitis and bilateral optic neuritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Differential Diagnosis: Essential to exclude conditions with similar presentations, especially multiple sclerosis (MS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Essential to exclude conditions with similar presentations, especially multiple sclerosis (MS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biomarker Testing: AQP4-IgG antibodies are specific to NMOSD and present in most cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AQP4-IgG antibodies are specific to NMOSD and present in most cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characterized by transverse myelitis and bilateral optic neuritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Essential to exclude conditions with similar presentations, especially multiple sclerosis (MS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AQP4-IgG antibodies are specific to NMOSD and present in most cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Magnetic Resonance Key for visualizing brain and spinal cord lesions, ruling Imaging (MRI) out other causes."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Brain MRI: Can show high-signal lesions, typically in periventricular areas. (Option D ruled out) Spinal MRI: Reveals lesions over three spinal segments, distinguishing NMOSD from MS."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cerebrospinal Fluid Lumbar puncture to assess inflammation and rule out (CSF) Analysis other conditions."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pleocytosis: Increased lymphocytes may be observed. Oligoclonal Bands: Less common in NMOSD compared to MS. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Evoked Potentials It measures the brain's electrical activity in response to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"stimuli; it is less used with MRI advancement. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Exclusion Tests Rule out infections, vitamin B12 deficiency, and spinal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cord compression via blood tests."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A medicine resident describes a typical case of a 35-year-old woman who began experiencing intermittent visual disturbances, with painful eye movement and muscle weakness. After an MRI showed lesions in her brain, the diagnosis of multiple sclerosis was confirmed. He explains that these lesions occur because certain cells cross the blood-brain barrier and mistakenly attack the myelin around the nerves in the central nervous system. Which cells is he describing?","o":[{"t":"Oligodendrocytes"},{"t":"T lymphocytes"},{"t":"Microglia"},{"t":"Astrocyte cells"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) T lymphocytes"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: T lymphocytes, particularly autoreactive CD4+ T cells, are crucial in the pathogenesis of multiple sclerosis. They are responsible for crossing the blood-brain barrier and initiating an immune response against myelin, leading to demyelination and the formation of lesions in the CNS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is an autoimmune disease of the central nervous system characterized by chronic inflammation, demyelination, gliosis (plaques or scarring), and neuronal loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The course can be relapsing or progressive."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MS plaques typically develop at different times and in different CNS locations."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both genetic and environmental factors are contributing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Environmental factors: sunlight exposure, vitamin D deficiency (controversial), and Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) exposure, though mechanisms are unclear"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic factors: 15% familial occurrence risk, highest in first-degree relatives; 30% concordance in monozygotic twins"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immune hypothesis: increased T lymphocytes in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and higher immunoglobulin synthesis in the CNS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood-brain barrier (BBB) injury: Activated T lymphocytes cross the blood-brain barrier, recognize myelin antigens on nervous system antigen-presenting cells (microglia) (Option C ruled out) and proliferate (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activated T lymphocytes cross the blood-brain barrier, recognize myelin antigens on nervous system antigen-presenting cells (microglia) (Option C ruled out) and proliferate (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demyelination: Inflammatory cascade releases cytokines, leading to oligodendrocyte –myelin destruction by macrophages Lesions: Disintegration of myelin, lymphocyte/macrophage infiltration, and perivascular inflammation Ectopic lymphoid follicles are linked to demyelination and neuronal loss in the cerebral cortex Diffusible factors from these lymphoid cells may cause demyelination and neurodegeneration"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory cascade releases cytokines, leading to oligodendrocyte –myelin destruction by macrophages"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions: Disintegration of myelin, lymphocyte/macrophage infiltration, and perivascular inflammation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic lymphoid follicles are linked to demyelination and neuronal loss in the cerebral cortex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffusible factors from these lymphoid cells may cause demyelination and neurodegeneration"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Axonal damage: Demyelination reduces trophic support for axons, redistributes ion channels, and destabilizes action potential membrane potentials Neuronal and axonal death may result from glutamate excitotoxicity, oxidative injury, iron accumulation, and mitochondrial failure Inflammatory mediators like nitric oxide initiate and worsen axonal damage Axons can adapt initially to these injuries, but over time, distal and retrograde degeneration (“dying-back” axonopathy) occurs Axonal loss accumulates over time due to repeated attacks and lack of neurotrophic factors from oligodendrocytes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demyelination reduces trophic support for axons, redistributes ion channels, and destabilizes action potential membrane potentials"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuronal and axonal death may result from glutamate excitotoxicity, oxidative injury, iron accumulation, and mitochondrial failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory mediators like nitric oxide initiate and worsen axonal damage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Axons can adapt initially to these injuries, but over time, distal and retrograde degeneration (“dying-back” axonopathy) occurs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Axonal loss accumulates over time due to repeated attacks and lack of neurotrophic factors from oligodendrocytes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plaque formation: After acute attacks, gliosis occurs by astrocyte cells, forming scars (Option D ruled out) Surviving oligodendrocytes partially remyelinate axons causing shadow plaques (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"After acute attacks, gliosis occurs by astrocyte cells, forming scars (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surviving oligodendrocytes partially remyelinate axons causing shadow plaques (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oligodendrocyte precursor cells often fail to differentiate into myelin-producing cells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Long-term myelin loss leads to reduced impulse propagation and conduction block, impairing CNS functions"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activated T lymphocytes cross the blood-brain barrier, recognize myelin antigens on nervous system antigen-presenting cells (microglia) (Option C ruled out) and proliferate (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory cascade releases cytokines, leading to oligodendrocyte –myelin destruction by macrophages"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions: Disintegration of myelin, lymphocyte/macrophage infiltration, and perivascular inflammation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic lymphoid follicles are linked to demyelination and neuronal loss in the cerebral cortex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffusible factors from these lymphoid cells may cause demyelination and neurodegeneration"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demyelination reduces trophic support for axons, redistributes ion channels, and destabilizes action potential membrane potentials"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuronal and axonal death may result from glutamate excitotoxicity, oxidative injury, iron accumulation, and mitochondrial failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory mediators like nitric oxide initiate and worsen axonal damage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Axons can adapt initially to these injuries, but over time, distal and retrograde degeneration (“dying-back” axonopathy) occurs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Axonal loss accumulates over time due to repeated attacks and lack of neurotrophic factors from oligodendrocytes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"After acute attacks, gliosis occurs by astrocyte cells, forming scars (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surviving oligodendrocytes partially remyelinate axons causing shadow plaques (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8422}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 30-year-old Asian woman presents to the emergency department with sudden bilateral vision changes and progressive weakness in her limbs. She has also experienced severe headaches and persistent nausea over the past week. Laboratory tests reveal positive IgG antibodies against aquaporin-4. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological process occurring in her condition?","o":[{"t":"Anti-AQP4 antibodies directly kill neurons"},{"t":"Anti-AQP4 antibodies cause astrocyte damage via complement activation"},{"t":"AQP4 antibodies block oligodendrocyte function"},{"t":"AQP4 antibodies create a toxic cerebrospinal fluid environment"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Anti-AQP4 antibodies cause astrocyte damage via complement activation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This case is indicative of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD), where the presence of anti-AQP4 antibodies leads to astrocyte injury and inflammation through complement-mediated mechanisms, resulting in demyelination and neuronal damage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-AQP4 Autoantibodies produced peripherally. fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Enter Central Nervous System (CNS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bind to Astrocyte foot processes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Induce Complement-mediated cell damage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Granulocyte infiltration"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Astrocyte death"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increase in IL-6 levels in CNS"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inflammation in the brain"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary death of oligodendrocytes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Demyelination"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neuronal cell death"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Local CNS water imbalance"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Further oligodendrocyte damage and demyelination"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 29-year-old African woman presents with a sudden onset of blurred vision in both eyes and weakness in her arms and legs. She reports a recent episode of severe headaches and nausea. Neurological examination reveals significant visual impairment and marked upper and lower extremity weakness. Serum testing shows the presence of IgG antibodies against aquaporin-4. Where are the aquaporin-4 antibodies primarily concentrated in the central nervous system?","o":[{"t":"Area postrema"},{"t":"Spinal cord"},{"t":"Circumventricular areas"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) All of the above"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This case is of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD) due to the presence of IgG antibodies against aquaporin-4, which is a hallmark of the disease. The patient's symptoms—sudden onset of blurred vision, weakness in the arms and legs, and a recent episode of severe headaches and nausea—align with common clinical presentations of NMOSD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms—sudden onset of blurred vision, weakness in the arms and legs, and a recent episode of severe headaches and nausea—align with common clinical presentations of NMOSD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aquaporin-4 antibodies are primarily concentrated in several key areas of the central nervous system, including the spinal cord, area postrema, and circumventricular areas (such as the periaqueductal gray matter)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms—sudden onset of blurred vision, weakness in the arms and legs, and a recent episode of severe headaches and nausea—align with common clinical presentations of NMOSD. Aspect Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Autoantibody IgG antibodies against aquaporin-4 (AQP4)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevalence Present in 60% to 90% of NMOSD patients"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Specificity 90%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sensitivity 70% to 90%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"AQP4 function Transmembrane water channel on astrocyte foot"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"processes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Concentration Areas Optic nerve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Spinal cord Area postrema Circumventricular areas (e.g., periaqueductal gray matter)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Role in NMOSD Autoimmune demyelinating astrocytopathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechanism of Action AQP4-IgG mediates perivascular lymphocytic infiltration"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Outcome Leads to axonal loss with a preference for areas rich in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"AQP4"}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 38-year-old African woman presents with four months of progressive bilateral visual loss and temporal headaches. Neurological examination reveals flaccid paraplegia and sensory loss below T4. Serum testing shows the presence of aquaporin-4 antibodies. Which of the following MRI findings is incorrect for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Longitudinally extensive lesions in the spinal cord are found with ‡3 vertebral segments."},{"t":"Optic nerve lesions are typically short and localized."},{"t":"Brain MRI may show peri-ependymal lesions around the ventricular system"},{"t":"Normal-appearing tissue is limited to lesional tracts and associated cortex."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Optic nerve lesions are typically short and localized."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is a case of Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD). The patient's presentation of progressive bilateral visual loss, temporal headaches, and neurological deficits, alongside the detection of aquaporin-4 antibodies , strongly indicates NMOSD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation of progressive bilateral visual loss, temporal headaches, and neurological deficits, alongside the detection of aquaporin-4 antibodies , strongly indicates NMOSD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In NMOSD, optic nerve lesions are often long and can involve the posterior chiasm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation of progressive bilateral visual loss, temporal headaches, and neurological deficits, alongside the detection of aquaporin-4 antibodies , strongly indicates NMOSD."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Location Characteristics"},{"k":"img","v":8430},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Spinal Cord"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":8435},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Optic Nerve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Long-length /posterior-chiasmal lesions. ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":8441},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Brain"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Others Normal-appearing tissue involvement is limited to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lesional tracts and associated cortex (Option D) Reduced lesional myo-inositol on MRS."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 36-year-old Caucasian woman presents with a history of limb weakness, gait unsteadiness, and paresthesias. She experiences an electric shock sensation that radiates down her spine when flexing her neck. An MRI of the brain is given below. What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"High-dose IV corticosteroids"},{"t":"Oral dimethyl fumarate"},{"t":"Ocrelizumab"},{"t":"Baclofen"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) High-dose IV corticosteroids"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This case is consistent with Multiple Sclerosis (Acute exacerbation) Because of the patient's symptoms, including limb weakness, gait unsteadiness, paresthesias, and Lhermitte sign. The MRI findings of multiple periventricular white matter lesions further support the diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Because of the patient's symptoms, including limb weakness, gait unsteadiness, paresthesias, and Lhermitte sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The MRI findings of multiple periventricular white matter lesions further support the diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-dose IV corticosteroids are the most appropriate treatment for acute exacerbations of MS, as they reduce inflammation and accelerate recovery from attacks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Because of the patient's symptoms, including limb weakness, gait unsteadiness, paresthesias, and Lhermitte sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The MRI findings of multiple periventricular white matter lesions further support the diagnosis. Treatment Category Interventions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Acute Exacerbations High-dose IV corticosteroids"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"first-line treatment for acute flares (Option A) Plasma exchange for patients who do not respond to corticosteroids."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease-Modifying Therapies Natalizumab and Ocrelizumab are highly effective for (Option B, C) patients who haven’t responded to other therapies."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Natalizumab: blocks integrin a4, preventing leukocyte adhesion to endothelial cells. It has a risk of PML Ocrelizumab: targets and depletes CD20-positive B cells. Patients with less active disease may be started on oral medications like Dimethyl fumarate or Fingolimod (sphingosine-1-phosphate modulator) b-interferon and Glatiramer have the highest safety profiles but lower effectiveness."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptomatic Therapy Baclofen"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"used for spasticity (Option D) Cholinergic for urinary retention Anticholinergics for urinary incontinence Carbamazepine, amitriptyline for painful paresthesias Antidepressants Neurogenic bladder treatments Catheterization, muscarinic antagonists, botulinum toxin injection Spasticity treatments (GABA B receptor agonists)"}],"qi":[8397]},{"n":16,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a 2-day history of acute, painful vision loss in her left eye, associated with a dull ache that worsens with eye movement. She also reports episodes of leg weakness, difficulty maintaining balance, and tingling sensations in her arms. Her medical history includes recurrent episodes of blurred vision and fatigue, particularly worsening in warm conditions. What is the diagnostic test of choice for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Visual evoked potential"},{"t":"Lumbar puncture"},{"t":"MRI of the brain and spinal cord"},{"t":"Blood test for oligoclonal bands"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) MRI of the brain and spinal cord"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is a case of Multiple Sclerosis (MS), characterized by episodes of neurological symptoms. The patient's symptoms, including acute vision loss, weakness, and sensory disturbances, are consistent with MS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI of the brain and spinal cord is the diagnostic test of choice because it can effectively identify the characteristic lesions associated with MS, particularly in the periventricular regions and spinal cord"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Description"},{"k":"img","v":8445},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MRI"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Imaging modality of choice for MS Shows multiple asymmetric, periventricular white matter lesions (Dawson fingers) Red arrowheads referred to as Dawson nngers ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Visual Evoked Potential Assesses optic nerve damage (Option A) CSF Analysis ›IgG index indicates intrathecal synthesis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Agarose electrophoresis: > 2 oligoclonal bands (not found in serum) (Option D) Lumbar puncture: can provide supportive evidence (Option B) > 5 cells/microliter; protein > 1g, cells > 75 suggests alternative diagnosis"}]},{"n":17,"q":"In a person with 2 or more attacks and clinical evidence of 2 or more lesions, what additional criteria are required for a diagnosis of Multiple Sclerosis?","o":[{"t":"None, dissemination in space and in time are met"},{"t":"Presence of enhancing and non-enhancing lesions on MRI"},{"t":"Presence of CSF oligoclonal bands"},{"t":"New T2 or enhancing MRI lesion compared to baseline"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) None; dissemination in space and in time are met"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a person with 2 or more attacks and clinical evidence of 2 or more lesions, both dissemination in space (DIS) and dissemination in time (DIT) have already been established."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIS is confirmed because multiple lesions exist in the central nervous system (CNS) regions"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIT is confirmed because multiple clinical attacks have occurred over time. Therefore, no additional criteria are necessary to make the diagnosis of MS in this scenario"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2017 McDonald Criteria for the Diagnosis of MS (Multiple Sclerosis)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical presentation Additional criteria to make MS diagnosis …in a person who has experienced a typical attack/CIS at onset"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2 or more attacks and clinical evidence of 2 or more None, DIS and DIT have been met (Option A) lesions; OR 2 or more attacks and clinical evidence of 1 lesion with clear historical evidence of prior attack involving lesion in different location"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2 or more attacks and clinical evidence of 1 lesion DIS shown by one of these criteria:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- additional clinical attack implicating different CNS site - 1 or more MS-typical T2 lesions in 2 or more areas of CNS: periventricular, cortical, juxtacortical, infratentorial, or spinal cord"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1 attack and clinical evidence of 2 or more lesions DIT shown by one of these criteria:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Additional clinical attack - Simultaneous presence of both enhancing and non-enhancing MS typical MRI lesions, or new T2 or enhancing MRI lesion compared to baseline scan (without regard to timing of baseline scan) (Options B & D ruled out) - CSF oligoclonal bands (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1 attack and clinical evidence of 1 lesion DIS shown by one of these criteria:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Additional attack implicating different CNS site - 1 or more MS-typical T2 lesions in 2 or more areas of CNS: periventricular, cortical, juxtacortical, infratentorial, or spinal cord AND DIT shown by one of these criteria: - additional clinical attack - Simultaneous presence of both enhancing and non-enhancing MS typical MRI lesions, or new T2 or enhancing MRI lesion compared to baseline scan (without regard to timing of baseline scan) - CSF oligoclonal bands"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"…in a person who has a steady progression of the disease since the onset"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1 year of disease progression (retrospective or DIS shown by at least two of these criteria: prospective) - 1 or more MS-typical T2 lesions (periventricular,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cortical, juxtacortical, or infratentorial) - 2 or more T2 spinal cord lesions - CSF oligoclonal bands"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abbreviations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIT = dissemination in time"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIS = dissemination in space"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS = central nervous system"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF = cerebrospinal fluid"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T2 lesion = hyperintense lesion on T2 weighted MRI"}]},{"n":18,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding One and a Half Syndrome in multiple sclerosis is not true?","o":[{"t":"It is characterized by ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy"},{"t":"Limited adduction of the ipsilateral eye"},{"t":"The contralateral eye exhibits exotropia and abduction nystagmus."},{"t":"Vertical gaze and convergence are typically impaired in this syndrome."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Vertical gaze and convergence are typically impaired in this syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vertical gaze and convergence are preserved in one-and-a-half syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"One and a Half Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Combination of ipsilateral conjugate horizontal gaze palsy (the \"one\") and ipsilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) (the \"half\")"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Physiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual signals from frontal and parietal lobes activate the Paramedian Pontine Reticular Formation (PPRF)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPRF excites the ipsilateral abducens nucleus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abducens nucleus sends: Motor fibres to the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle Signals to the contralateral MLF activate the medial rectus of the opposite eye via the oculomotor nucleus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor fibres to the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signals to the contralateral MLF activate the medial rectus of the opposite eye via the oculomotor nucleus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor fibres to the ipsilateral lateral rectus muscle"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signals to the contralateral MLF activate the medial rectus of the opposite eye via the oculomotor nucleus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Possible lesion sites responsible for the horizontal gaze palsy in One and a Half Syndrome include: Damage to both the ipsilateral abducens nucleus and the PPRF. Damage confined to the ipsilateral abducens nucleus. Damage isolated to the ipsilateral PPRF. Damage to the ipsilateral abducens nerve root along with the contralateral MLF, potentially due to two separate lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to both the ipsilateral abducens nucleus and the PPRF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage confined to the ipsilateral abducens nucleus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage isolated to the ipsilateral PPRF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to the ipsilateral abducens nerve root along with the contralateral MLF, potentially due to two separate lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to both the ipsilateral abducens nucleus and the PPRF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage confined to the ipsilateral abducens nucleus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage isolated to the ipsilateral PPRF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to the ipsilateral abducens nerve root along with the contralateral MLF, potentially due to two separate lesions."},{"k":"img","v":8453},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Presentation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Limited adduction of the ipsilateral eye (INO). (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral eye: Exotropia with abduction nystagmus. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Preserved vertical gaze and convergence. (Option D)"},{"k":"img","v":8458}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 45-year-old man with a known diagnosis of multiple sclerosis presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset double vision. On examination, the left eye has slowed adduction when attempting to look to the right, and there is horizontal nystagmus in the right eye when it is abducting. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathology of this patient’s symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Lesion in the right lateral rectus muscle"},{"t":"Lesion in the left sixth cranial nerve"},{"t":"Lesion in the left medial longitudinal fasciculus"},{"t":"Lesion in the right sixth cranial nerve"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Lesion in the left medial longitudinal fasciculus"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the history of multiple sclerosis and clinical manifestation the most probable diagnosis is internuclear ophthalmoplegia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the lesion in the left medial longitudinal fasciculus interrupts communication between the left third cranial nerve nucleus (responsible for adducting the left eye) and the right sixth cranial nerve nucleus (responsible for abducting the right eye). This results in slowed adduction of the left eye when attempting to look to the right and horizontal nystagmus in the right eye as it abducts."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is an ocular movement disorder caused by a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Others Trauma Tentorial herniation Infections (like HIV, syphilis, cysticercosis, herpes zoster, tuberculoma) Tumors (medulloblastoma, glioma, lymphoma, metastases) Vasculitis (systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), Sjogren syndrome) Iatrogenic injury Brainstem haemorrhage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Trauma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tentorial herniation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infections (like HIV, syphilis, cysticercosis, herpes zoster, tuberculoma)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tumors (medulloblastoma, glioma, lymphoma, metastases)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vasculitis (systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), Sjogren syndrome)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iatrogenic injury"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brainstem haemorrhage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Trauma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tentorial herniation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infections (like HIV, syphilis, cysticercosis, herpes zoster, tuberculoma)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tumors (medulloblastoma, glioma, lymphoma, metastases)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vasculitis (systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), Sjogren syndrome)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iatrogenic injury"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brainstem haemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Physiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When a person looks on the left side, the lateral rectus muscle supplied by the 6th nerve and the medial rectus muscle supplied by the 3rd nerve gets in sync with each other so that eyes can fixate on a particular object."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cross-communication occurs with the help of the medial longitudinal fasciculus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), blocks the communication between the contralateral sixth nerve nucleus and ipsilateral third nerve nucleus, affecting horizontal gaze (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Slowing or impaired adduction in the ipsilateral eye to the MLF lesion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Horizontal nystagmus in the abducting eye (contralateral eye) as a compensatory response"},{"k":"img","v":8465},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lesion in the right lateral rectus muscle (Option A): It leads to weakness in the abduction of the right eye. However, this does not cause slowed adduction of the left eye or the horizontal nystagmus observed in the right eye. Therefore, this option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lesion in the left sixth cranial nerve (Option B): It leads to the inability to abduct the left eye, resulting in double vision when looking to the left. In this scenario, the patient has slowed adduction in the left eye and double vision when looking to the right. Therefore, this option is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lesion in the right sixth cranial nerve (Option D): It leads to the inability to abduct the right eye, resulting in double vision when looking to the right. In this scenario, the patient has slowed adduction in the left eye. Therefore, this option is incorrect."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 34-year-old woman with a known diagnosis of multiple sclerosis presents with complaints of increased fatigue and worsening of her neurological symptoms after spending a day in the sauna. Her vision becomes blurry, and her leg weakness worsens. Which of the following best describes her experience?","o":[{"t":"Lhermitte’s symptoms"},{"t":"Uthoff’s symptoms"},{"t":"Optic neuritis"},{"t":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Uthoff’s symptoms"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Uthoff's symptom refers to the temporary worsening of neurological symptoms due to increased body temperature, often triggered by heat exposure, such as spending time in a sauna. This symptom is common in patients with multiple sclerosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) Clinical features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abrupt or insidious onset of symptoms Sensory symptoms Paresthesias: tingling, prickling sensations, “pins and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"needles,” or painful burning. Hypesthesia: reduced sensation, numbness, or a “dead” feeling. Unpleasant sensations: feelings that body parts are swollen, wet, raw, or tightly wrapped. Pain is a common symptom of MS."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Optic neuritis (ON) (Option Diminished visual acuity. C ruled out) Decreased colour perception in the central field of vision."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"May progress to severe visual loss. Periorbital pain. Fundoscopic examination: normal or optic disc swelling (papillitis), pallor of the optic disc (optic atrophy)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Weakness of the limbs Loss of strength, speed, dexterity, and gait disturbance."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Exercise-induced weakness is a characteristic symptom of MS. Upper motor neuron type weakness accompanied by other pyramidal signs."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Facial weakness Lesion in the pons may resemble idiopathic Bell’s palsy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Spasticity Spontaneous and movement-induced muscle spasms."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Painful spasms."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Visual blurring Due to ON (optic neuritis) or diplopia."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diplopia is caused by internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) or palsy of the sixth cranial nerve. (Option D ruled out) Bilateral INO is particularly suggestive of MS. Other gaze disturbances in MS: Horizontal gaze palsy One-and-a-half syndrome (horizontal gaze palsy plus an INO) Acquired pendular nystagmus"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ataxia Cerebellar tremors."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cerebellar dysarthria."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vertigo Lesion in the brainstem."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ancillary symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal symptoms Brief duration (10s to 2 min), high frequency (5–40"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"episodes per day), no change of consciousness or change in background electroencephalogram during episodes, and a self-limited course."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lhermitte’s symptom (Option A ruled out) An electric shock-like sensation that radiates down the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"back into the legs. Typically induced by flexion or other movements of the neck."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Trigeminal neuralgia, hemifacial spasm, Demyelinating lesions involve the root entry or exit zone and glossopharyngeal neuralgia of the fifth, seventh, and ninth cranial nerve, respectively."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Facial myokymia Persistent rapid flickering contractions of the facial"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"musculature Slow spread of contraction across the face."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heat sensitivity Uthoff symptoms: Neurological symptoms develop from"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"heat exposure, such as steam baths and saunas. (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bladder dysfunction Present in >90% of MS patients."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Detrusor hyperreflexia. Detrusor sphincter dyssynergia - hesitancy, urinary retention, overflow incontinence, and recurrent infection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bowel dysfunction Constipation, faecal urgency or bowel incontinence."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cognitive dysfunction Memory loss."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Impaired attention."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Psychiatric symptom Depression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fatigue 90% of patients experience fatigue."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It causes work-related disability."}]}]);
